# The Liberation Movement Must Not Be Obstructed
Tamil title: விடுதலை இயக்கம் தடைபடக் கூடாது

The following is a compilation of advice given by Arignar Anna at a meeting on the Madras beach on 16 April 1961 concerning the condition of the Kazhagam. The OCR prints the year as 1967, but the departure of E. V. K. Sampath and the internal chronology establish 1961.

Mathiazhagan, who spoke before me, described the victory we have achieved and the victory we shall achieve. I openly acknowledge the defeat I have suffered. Until yesterday my heart was full of confidence that I could remove any discord that arose within the Kazhagam. Today it has left an indelible scar upon my heart. I have suffered the greatest defeat of my public life.

There may be several defects in administration. Whoever conducts it, those defects must be removed at the proper time; one must possess the ability to remove them and be given the time required. If these three things are absent, this camp, built through immense labour, will—as friend Mathiazhagan said—become a target for our opponents' talk.

I accept the news that Sampath and others have left as a lesson that we must acquire still greater administrative ability. With courage and a pure heart, I continue to believe that the departing comrades will return and rejoin us.

Karunanidhi said certain newspaper reporters were attempting to scatter this movement. There is no use blaming those reporters. Their employers may benefit when they publish sensational news.

If a highly skilled artist draws nude portraits of his own mother and sister, no one will regard him as a good artist. Karunanidhi at least said he had admitted reporters as far as the veranda of his house. I do not allow them even nearby.

When a meeting occurs, the newspapers carry no report. Tens of thousands of you have gathered here. Mathiazhagan spoke of the Kazhagam with historical evidence; Karunanidhi spoke with inner passion. None of their speeches will appear in tomorrow's newspapers. Hundreds of thousands of talented and enthusiastic people have gathered here, but the press will print nothing about any of you.

If a pig came here, they would print it under an eight-column headline in large type: “Pig at Annadurai Meeting; Commotion in Crowd.” Thus they would uphold journalistic ethics. I do not care what is written by people who cannot distinguish much between a human being and a pig.

When I was recently in Nagercoil, a reporter came and asked, “What news will you give?” I asked, “To whom?” He replied, “To your Kazhagam comrades.” I said, “At the right time, I shall give our comrades the proper news through the proper channel. Do not worry.” He again asked what news I would give. I told him to find a good profession.

We therefore need not worry about reporters. The press did not create the discord; it exploited what occurred.

If those who left did so because they desired a little more purity and ability, I shall bear the pain and try to remove the defects. I still do not believe they left merely to create confusion.

Someone wearing earrings set with diamonds worth fifty thousand rupees will remove them when a sore develops in the ear and wear them again after it heals. Likewise, if younger brother Sampath tells me what wound exists, I shall try to heal it. I ask him to wait until it heals. I tell this to Sampath and to the people. Until that wound heals, it is the duty of those who left to protect the Kazhagam.

For another month, Congress people will enter and conduct their “divine games.” They will publish stories saying, “Ten people have left the Kazhagam; ten more may leave tomorrow,” and the next day, “Those ten too have left.” For another month, the Kazhagam may be good hunting for every newspaper. They may celebrate Deepavali while we weep. But these tears will not leave them undisturbed.

The DMK may contain several defects. Why have I not removed them? When do you give me enough rest to do so?

From the first to the fifth of this month I participated in four or five meetings each day in Thanjavur district. On the sixth I reached Kanchipuram. On the morning of the seventh, there was a wedding in Kanchi. That evening I attended a public meeting at Thennneri and was returning to Madras when my car broke down. Had another car been available, I would have attended the publication celebration conducted by friend Parthasarathy for the *History of the DMK*.

One child is crying, another stretches its legs in a cradle, and a third has been in the womb for six months. In that condition, what use is there in becoming angry with the wife if she serves undercooked rice? An understanding husband will realise that he has burdened her with too much work. Likewise, you have worked me extremely hard for the past twenty-five years.

Whenever weariness comes upon me, I think of you. You proclaim “Dravida Nadu for the Dravidians” and are prepared to make any sacrifice to attain Dravidam. How can I display my weariness to you? If I had adequate time, removing the defects would not be impossible.

A wife combs her hair, adorns it with flowers and prepares to go to the bazaar with her husband. But the comb is missing four or five teeth, and the silvering has gone from the mirror so that her face cannot be seen. What can she do? The husband waiting at the doorway will naturally become angry. Would he like her to set out untidily because she could not comb her hair? Likewise, if certain administrative matters are displeasing, time is needed to correct them.

I do not wish to deceive you by saying that the bitterness among us can easily be concealed. An ordinary stomach ache may end after drinking soda. If not, ginger beer may cure it. If that too fails, it means a disorder exists in the stomach and some medicine must be taken. I therefore state that my friends and I will attempt to remove these defects.

Meanwhile, some may say Sampath will join Congress. If he could not tolerate the ordinary bad smell within his own house after seeing a few defects here, how could he accept and endure the sewer within Congress? How could he shake hands with Congress? He did not leave to support capitalism or restore Congress to office.

Until this very second, I have believed he left only to give me greater urgency. For the sake of argument—not as a statement of fact—let us say it was already clear at that time that this was indeed a split.

## Appended Historical Segment: Edward II and Parliament

[At this point the available scans abruptly change subject and typography. The following historical text appears to have been concatenated from another work.]

Edward II was devoted to pleasure. He was known for extravagant expenditure, vulgar amusements, drinking and gambling. Drunkards, gamblers, entertainers and court dwarfs constantly surrounded him. The king made some members of this circle nobles and others ministers. Capable people from Edward I's reign were removed, and spendthrifts useful to the king's pleasures took office. Expenditure increased, Government funds emptied, and the people's suffering and poverty grew. Yet the king and Gaveston, who assisted in flattering his pleasures, turned the palace and Government into a great entertainment hall.

### The People Correct the King

Parliament, meeting in April 1308, openly condemned the king's conduct. Its members declared, “Our submission and reverence belong to the Crown, not to the individual who happens to be king. If the king behaves wrongly, the people have a responsibility to return him to the right path.”

Parliament insisted that Gaveston, the accomplice in the king's misconduct, be immediately banished. The king initially submitted and sent Gaveston to Ireland. Parliament had acquired the courage to command a king, “Expel the man you favour from the country,” and the king obeyed.

Unable to remain apart from Gaveston, the king petitioned the Parliament meeting in 1309 for permission to recall him. What a pitiable condition: to invite his preferred friend into a country under his own rule, the king had to petition Parliament. He submitted the petition; Parliament rejected it.

Ashamed and distressed, the king dissolved Parliament and recalled Gaveston. He tried to divide some nobles and bring them to his side, believing he could manage the situation. But Gaveston's arrogance and authority again made many people enemies.

Parliament met and issued a demand. It established a governing committee like the one appointed in 1258, and the king had to accept it. Conflict arose between the committee and Gaveston, the ruler of revelry. He was ultimately captured and beheaded.

A resolution passed by Parliament in January 1315 clearly shows the extent to which it restricted royal rights.

“Many people attached to the royal household are unnecessary. Because they burden both king and country, they must be removed,” Parliament resolved. It decided to expel the pleasure-seeking crowd that rioted within the palace. No earlier Parliament had contemplated such a measure, and no king had received such advice.

### Ten Pounds

An even more mocking resolution followed. With great generosity, Parliament resolved to allocate the king an allowance of ten pounds per day for his expenses. Like giving a labourer his daily wage, Parliament gave the king a daily allowance: ten pounds a day, with no expenditure beyond it.

The allocation carried another condition. A committee was appointed to spend and supervise the money on the king's behalf. If the king needed something, if the need was reasonable and total expenditure remained within the permitted amount, the parliamentary committee would authorise it. Parliament gave him ten pounds a day and retained supervision over how even that ten pounds was spent.

One historian says that “the humiliation of King Edward II reached its highest point in the Parliament that met in London in January 1315.” The king was regarded as an employee or office-holder working within the Government.

During the thirteenth century, the foundations necessary for Parliament were laid. The Magna Carta, promises obtained from kings, Simon's Parliament and the Model Parliament all belonged to this period. In that century, the institution called Parliament, containing representatives of the people, took shape. In the fourteenth century, Parliament obtained rights to control both king and Government.

### The House of Commons and the House of Lords

Parliament contains two institutions, an upper and a lower house. The upper house became the House of Lords and the lower the House of Commons.

Before the name Parliament arose, when it was a great council, everyone assembled in one chamber. As long as it remained an advisory council accepting the king's decisions, there was no need for divisions. Later, as groups gradually acquired necessity and concern for protecting their respective rights, the divisions became permanent institutions.

Those summoned to Parliament belonged to three classes: religious figures, nobles and common people. The religious leaders and senior clergy did not sit with everyone else in the assembly. They created a separate ecclesiastical council and made decisions there. They themselves determined the taxes payable to the Government. Until the middle of the seventeenth century, they possessed the authority to tax religious property.

Because the clergy formed their separate council, two divisions remained in Parliament: nobles on one side and representatives of counties and boroughs on the other. Those representatives were mainly merchants and knights. The knights naturally sat among the nobles, but merchants hesitated to sit opposite them. The assembly's arrangement and the nobles' appearance intimidated them. Although some merchants possessed more money than the nobles, they were not regarded as equally elevated in society. The common representatives felt more discomfort than the nobles about sitting together.

By 1340, the practice of lords and commoners assembling separately had become definite. Religious leaders and senior clergy, regarded as lords, joined the House of Lords. At first, knights possessed a right to join the Lords and an opportunity to join the Commons. They initially could not decide which side to support, but later chose to sit in the Commons.

Lords and religious leaders sat in the House of Lords; common representatives and knights sat in the House of Commons. Such a political structure may be said to have emerged by the middle of the fourteenth century.

Those sitting in the Lords were born into noble families, had received noble titles or were high-ranking clergy. Representatives elected by boroughs and counties sat in the Commons. They were ordinary people or merchants.

In the early period, coming to Parliament did not seem an honour to them. Nor did they possess the consciousness that Parliament existed to protect popular rights. Unable to bear local pressure, they came reluctantly. Long travel and hardship along the way prevented many from attending. Even when they arrived, they crept like cats among the nobles and knights and waited only for the meeting to end so they could return home.

Many hesitated to serve as representatives and departed for London only under strong pressure. When Parliament was to meet, the assemblies that selected representatives hesitated as much as the representatives themselves. Each constituency had to pay its delegate an allowance of two shillings a day. Some constituency assemblies resented paying even that amount. “Must we incur this wasteful expense merely to tax ourselves?” they muttered.

For this reason, some boroughs and towns petitioned the king and Parliament for exemption from sending representatives and obtained approval. They imagined that receiving the exemption was a privilege.

[The available source ends here without returning to the 1961 speech.]

---NOTES---
The opening date is OCRed as 16 April 1967. E. V. K. Sampath left the DMK in April 1961, and references to the Kazhagam's internal crisis establish 16 April 1961 as the probable date. The source remains verbatim.

Images 1–5 contain the Anna speech. Midway through image 5, the source abruptly shifts into an unrelated historical account of Edward II and Parliament, continuing through image 9. The bilingual document preserves and translates both segments but labels the discontinuity rather than silently merging their authorship or argument.

The historical dates OCRed as “308,” “7258” and “7340” are contextually read as 1308, 1258 and 1340. These readings should be confirmed against the scans before citation.

The source uses the metaphor of a diamond earring removed while an ear wound heals to ask departing members to protect the organisation during temporary separation.
